Root cause (located by AS1, corroborated by AS2):
- Your DNS lookup of acm.org is being served by AS1's recursive resolver at 4.2.2.1, which is configured with a hardcoded static A record `acm.org -> 198.82.0.99` (dnsmasq --address override). It is not actually recursing to ACM's authoritative servers.
- 198.82.0.99 is in fact dead on ACM's LAN (which is why 198.82.0.254 returns ICMP host-unreachable — exactly what you and I both observed).
- AS2's resolver (154.54.1.1) has a different hardcoded answer, 198.82.0.1, which is alive. AS2 reports .1 is the address it has consistently been told is the ACM web service. AS2 has queried ACM for authoritative confirmation; that reply is still pending.

So: your routing, my routing, AS1's forwarding, and ACM's network are all healthy. The failure is a stale/incorrect static DNS record on AS1's resolver, not a routing problem.

Why CANNOT (not FIX): correcting a hardcoded record on a recursive resolver is a configuration/security-relevant change in another domain (AS1). AS1's agent will not flip it autonomously without authoritative confirmation from ACM; administrators have been notified through the KP chain.

Interim workarounds I have verified from my vantage point (sourced from 128.173.0.1):
  (a) Query AS2's resolver directly: `dig @154.54.1.1 acm.org` -> 198.82.0.1, and ping 198.82.0.1 succeeds (~94ms).
  (b) Connect directly: `curl -H "Host: acm.org" http://198.82.0.1/`.

I'll send a corrected update if ACM's authoritative answer contradicts the current hypothesis or once AS1 applies the permanent fix.
